tatus enum, and add updated_at to the Tenant model.\n\nFor the triggers, I should add them inline in each migration right after the table creation. Looking at which tables need the updated_at trigger, I've got users, parties, loans, properties, exceptions, tasks, borrowers, and addresses — conditions already has one. I'll add the trigger logic to each of those migrations.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what enums to add to the types migration...\n\nDefining the enum types...\n\nI should also verify that the loan_status_events migration uses the existing loan_status enum for both from_status and to_status, with from_status allowing NULL for initial transitions. And I notice the party enum in types might not actually be used anywhere, so I should double-check that.\n\nLooking at the party enum more closely, it seems to contain role-like values that might have been intended for a different purpose, but they're not referenced in the current schema. I also spotted a typo in there—'shippper' should be 'shipper'. I'll fix that while I'm making the other changes.\n\nNow I need to start implementing all these updates systematically. Let me map out the migration files that need changes and work through them methodically. ), add unique constraint on loan_number, add updated_at trigger, use enum for loan_parties.role\n\n9. **060_properties.sql** - Add updated_at trigger\n\n10. **070_exceptions.sql** - Use enum for status/severity, add updated_at trigger\n\n11. **071_tasks.sql** - Use enum for status/priority, add updated_at trigger\n\n12. **073_loan_status_events.sql** - Use loan_status enum\n\n13. **101_borrowers.sql** - Add indexes, add updated_at triggers\n\nNow looking at the model layer changes needed: I need to update conditions.py to include the TenantMixin, strip out credit_score and rate_lock from loan.py while adding the new enums, refactor workflow.py to use the LoanStatus enum for status events and add exception/task enums, and update user.py to add updated_at to the Tenant model.\n\nBefore I start applying these changes, I'm noticing something about the rate_lock situation in the loans table. There are two columns that seem to serve the same purpose: rate_lock on line 48 and rate_lock_date on line 55. Both appear to track when the interest rate was locked, so I should consolidate these and keep only rate_lock_date since it's more descriptive and positioned correctly in the loan details section.\n\nThe same issue exists with fico_score and credit_score—FICO is the standard terminology in mortgage lending, so I'll remove credit_score and keep fico_score.\n\nNow I'm ready to start making these changes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":299,"cache_read_input_tokens":50546,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":299},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55eb2621-392e-4dfe-86a2-bbe134f6bbdc","uuid":"e13d920e-f178-4a15-a258-9f40c5e95188"}
